9 #uuid "fb5a8c81-2643-51d7-9805-3937d851aee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1069 "q-321990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d2f4bec-f2bc-5622-80f4-e5a5ebaa94bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534999 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535004 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535004 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15857" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535004 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535004 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/33/33070b4b871552c4c45f21f0885cbce45ff65eb278cb000e68c91adfc2ab004c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $16$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(16,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41e7831f-c290-5b73-a0b9-fe7067c749d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535004 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=16.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535012 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58130b5b-5f37-553e-8991-9f3dd2a84005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535012 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535014 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bcab4b1-53e9-547b-81a4-3bf0881c104a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535014 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-321957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ca/ca3a73d411d7b766196a2bebf56f9d678aba54a4f2265ac08c36d0fb3fbf03b0.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e3d47e1-6723-5134-bd1d-2937862c014c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535016 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/37/37665aa7ea5388a1162ca68a1662aaa86d6369f083d7563dbf27c521c13a7c72.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $